1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,570
Imagine lying on the floor of an airplane.

2
00:00:03,510 --> 00:00:05,870
Suddenly, you weigh almost twice as much.

3
00:00:06,770 --> 00:00:08,930
Lifting your arm becomes difficult.

4
00:00:09,490 --> 00:00:11,370
Moving your head takes effort.

5
00:00:11,880 --> 00:00:15,280
Then, about twenty seconds
later... you are floating.

6
00:00:15,360 --> 00:00:16,680
Your arm has no weight.

7
00:00:16,760 --> 00:00:18,600
Your feet aren't touching the floor.

8
00:00:19,290 --> 00:00:22,890
You can push yourself away from the
ground and fly across the cabin.

9
00:00:23,560 --> 00:00:26,600
And then, gradually, the
floor starts pushing you back.

10
00:00:28,140 --> 00:00:29,800
This isn't science fiction.

11
00:00:30,560 --> 00:00:33,840
This is how humans simulate
weightlessness here on Earth.

12
00:00:34,410 --> 00:00:36,190
And the strangest part
is that the airplane

13
00:00:36,420 --> 00:00:38,910
never actually leaves Earth's gravity.

14
00:00:40,350 --> 00:00:42,150
You can't turn gravity off.

15
00:00:42,730 --> 00:00:46,410
The Earth is still pulling on the
airplane, just as strongly as ever.

16
00:00:46,890 --> 00:00:50,970
So how do you make somebody feel
weightless? You let them fall.

17
00:00:51,950 --> 00:00:55,030
If you jump off a chair, you
are briefly in free fall.

18
00:00:55,670 --> 00:00:58,930
You and everything around you are
accelerating downward together.

19
00:01:00,040 --> 00:01:03,020
The same principle works
with an entire airplane.

20
00:01:03,680 --> 00:01:06,120
Instead of dropping for a
fraction of a second, it

21
00:01:06,350 --> 00:01:09,240
follows a carefully controlled
ballistic trajectory.

22
00:01:10,250 --> 00:01:12,290
This is called a parabolic flight.

23
00:01:13,660 --> 00:01:18,760
Specially modified aircraft, such
as the Airbus A310 Zero G, fly these

24
00:01:18,990 --> 00:01:22,900
trajectories again and again to
create short periods of microgravity.

25
00:01:23,880 --> 00:01:27,320
The one flying today used to
carry German government officials.

26
00:01:28,160 --> 00:01:31,000
It replaced an older
Airbus A300, which flew

27
00:01:31,230 --> 00:01:33,760
these parabolas for almost eighteen years.

28
00:01:34,830 --> 00:01:37,730
And each parabola has
several distinct phases.

29
00:01:38,530 --> 00:01:42,270
First, level flight at about six
kilometres, with normal gravity.

30
00:01:43,170 --> 00:01:45,990
Then the pilots pull the
aircraft sharply upward.

31
00:01:46,710 --> 00:01:50,312
The acceleration rises to
about one point eight g.

32
00:01:50,392 --> 00:01:52,772
Flying this takes three pilots at once.

33
00:01:52,852 --> 00:01:55,852
One controls the nose, up and down.

34
00:01:55,932 --> 00:01:58,852
One keeps the aircraft steady sideways.

35
00:01:59,132 --> 00:02:00,552
And one handles the engines.

36
00:02:00,782 --> 00:02:05,892
They're experienced test pilots, from
France's national flight test centre.

37
00:02:06,822 --> 00:02:08,682
Then comes the crucial moment.

38
00:02:09,262 --> 00:02:11,842
The aircraft enters its
free-fall trajectory.

39
00:02:12,982 --> 00:02:16,889
Inside the cabin, people
experience roughly zero g.

40
00:02:17,002 --> 00:02:18,502
For about twenty seconds.

41
00:02:19,092 --> 00:02:21,132
Then the pilots pull out of the dive.

42
00:02:21,492 --> 00:02:25,532
And the passengers experience
about one point eight g again.

43
00:02:26,232 --> 00:02:28,132
Then the cycle starts over.

44
00:02:28,842 --> 00:02:32,322
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

45
00:02:32,942 --> 00:02:34,562
It really helps the channel.

46
00:02:36,932 --> 00:02:39,572
One point eight g is
easy to underestimate.

47
00:02:40,572 --> 00:02:43,572
At one g, the floor supports
you with your normal weight.

48
00:02:44,272 --> 00:02:47,972
At one point eight g, the forces
are almost twice as large.

49
00:02:48,672 --> 00:02:52,072
If you weigh eighty kilograms,
your mass hasn't changed.

50
00:02:52,732 --> 00:02:55,992
But as far as the floor and your
muscles are concerned, your body

51
00:02:56,222 --> 00:03:00,522
behaves roughly as if it weighed a
hundred and forty-four kilograms.

52
00:03:00,822 --> 00:03:02,282
Your arm feels heavier.

53
00:03:02,362 --> 00:03:04,422
Your legs feel heavier.

54
00:03:04,662 --> 00:03:06,382
And your head feels heavier.

55
00:03:06,682 --> 00:03:10,042
Even moving your head can
take noticeably more effort.

56
00:03:11,042 --> 00:03:14,702
That's why passengers don't normally
stand around during this phase.

57
00:03:15,382 --> 00:03:18,362
They lie down or brace
themselves against the floor.

58
00:03:20,592 --> 00:03:22,972
And then something extraordinary happens.

59
00:03:24,502 --> 00:03:27,742
The aircraft enters free
fall. The airplane is falling.

60
00:03:27,972 --> 00:03:29,642
And you are falling with it.

61
00:03:30,142 --> 00:03:33,442
Because you and the aircraft are
accelerating downward together,

62
00:03:33,602 --> 00:03:37,082
the floor no longer needs to push
you upward to support your weight.

63
00:03:37,882 --> 00:03:40,382
And that's what you
experience as weightlessness.

64
00:03:41,432 --> 00:03:43,112
Try lifting your arm.

65
00:03:43,612 --> 00:03:46,352
Normally, your muscles have
to overcome its weight.

66
00:03:46,532 --> 00:03:48,932
Now they barely have to do anything.

67
00:03:49,882 --> 00:03:53,122
A tiny movement can send your
arm drifting through the air.

68
00:03:53,702 --> 00:03:55,902
You can push yourself away from the floor.

69
00:03:55,982 --> 00:03:58,002
And suddenly you're flying.

70
00:04:00,422 --> 00:04:01,702
But there is a catch.

71
00:04:02,462 --> 00:04:06,802
Your brain has spent your entire life
learning how movement is supposed to work.

72
00:04:07,482 --> 00:04:10,222
And now you're giving it
completely different information.

73
00:04:11,522 --> 00:04:15,622
Your sense of orientation comes from
several systems working together.

74
00:04:16,682 --> 00:04:19,142
Your eyes tell you what
the world looks like.

75
00:04:19,602 --> 00:04:22,882
Your muscles and joints tell
your brain where your body is.

76
00:04:23,502 --> 00:04:25,722
And your vestibular
system, inside your inner

77
00:04:25,952 --> 00:04:29,262
ear, detects movement and
changes in orientation.

78
00:04:30,352 --> 00:04:35,452
Normally, these systems agree. You stand
on the floor. Your eyes see the room.

79
00:04:35,932 --> 00:04:38,352
Your muscles feel the
floor supporting you.

80
00:04:38,792 --> 00:04:41,092
Your inner ear knows which way is up.

81
00:04:42,352 --> 00:04:44,532
But in weightlessness, the rules change.

82
00:04:45,312 --> 00:04:47,592
You can rotate your body without falling.

83
00:04:48,052 --> 00:04:49,872
You can turn upside down.

84
00:04:50,492 --> 00:04:54,692
You can move in three dimensions without
your feet telling you where the floor is.

85
00:04:55,412 --> 00:04:57,912
And your eyes can see one
thing while your vestibular

86
00:04:58,142 --> 00:05:01,252
system is experiencing
something completely different.

87
00:05:02,052 --> 00:05:04,912
That sensory mismatch can
cause motion sickness.

88
00:05:05,732 --> 00:05:07,492
And that's why something
as simple as turning

89
00:05:07,722 --> 00:05:10,292
your head can suddenly
make you feel terrible.

90
00:05:12,702 --> 00:05:16,602
You might think the first thing you'd want
to do in weightlessness is move around.

91
00:05:17,742 --> 00:05:20,562
Turn around. Look behind you. Try a flip.

92
00:05:20,842 --> 00:05:22,502
Push yourself across the cabin.

93
00:05:23,092 --> 00:05:25,472
And that's exactly what can make you sick.

94
00:05:26,212 --> 00:05:28,652
Head movements are particularly
troublesome, because

95
00:05:28,882 --> 00:05:31,512
they strongly stimulate
the vestibular system.

96
00:05:32,472 --> 00:05:34,812
That's why people on parabolic
flights are taught to

97
00:05:35,042 --> 00:05:37,612
move carefully and avoid
unnecessary head movements.

98
00:05:37,842 --> 00:05:41,852
Anti-nausea medication may also be used.

99
00:05:42,482 --> 00:05:46,522
Because twenty seconds of weightlessness
sounds like an incredible amount of fun...

100
00:05:46,602 --> 00:05:49,162
until your stomach disagrees.

101
00:05:52,222 --> 00:05:56,692
NASA flew its own version for
decades, a converted KC-135.

102
00:05:57,342 --> 00:06:00,802
It earned a nickname that
says it all: the Vomit Comet.

103
00:06:01,762 --> 00:06:03,822
Here's the really interesting part.

104
00:06:03,902 --> 00:06:07,322
Why can't the airplane just
stay weightless for a minute?

105
00:06:08,102 --> 00:06:11,982
After all, gravity isn't
going anywhere. So why stop?

106
00:06:12,602 --> 00:06:14,542
The answer is height and speed.

107
00:06:15,812 --> 00:06:18,472
The aircraft pulls up
from about six kilometres.

108
00:06:19,392 --> 00:06:22,252
When the free-fall phase
begins, it's roughly seven point

109
00:06:22,482 --> 00:06:25,560
six kilometres above the
ground, travelling at around six

110
00:06:25,790 --> 00:06:29,112
hundred and fifty kilometres
per hour, and still climbing.

111
00:06:30,262 --> 00:06:35,072
It continues upward for a while, to a peak
of roughly eight point five kilometres.

112
00:06:35,302 --> 00:06:37,462
Then it starts coming down.

113
00:06:38,262 --> 00:06:42,302
The important thing is that the airplane
is in free fall during both parts.

114
00:06:42,382 --> 00:06:44,762
It's weightless while climbing.

115
00:06:44,862 --> 00:06:46,802
It's weightless near the top.

116
00:06:46,882 --> 00:06:49,202
And it's still weightless
while descending.

117
00:06:50,342 --> 00:06:54,762
The entire microgravity phase lasts
roughly twenty to twenty-two seconds.

118
00:06:54,842 --> 00:06:58,022
And then the pilots have
to start pulling out.

119
00:06:59,392 --> 00:07:01,472
Let's exaggerate the experiment.

120
00:07:02,332 --> 00:07:04,372
What if the pilots didn't pull out?

121
00:07:04,752 --> 00:07:06,832
What if they simply let
the airplane continue

122
00:07:07,062 --> 00:07:09,632
on its ballistic trajectory
for a full minute?

123
00:07:11,052 --> 00:07:14,852
At first, it would climb. Then
it would reach its highest point.

124
00:07:15,092 --> 00:07:17,692
Then it would descend faster and faster.

125
00:07:18,722 --> 00:07:22,082
After about thirty-six seconds,
a simple ballistic calculation

126
00:07:22,312 --> 00:07:26,422
puts it back at six kilometres,
where it started pulling up.

127
00:07:27,022 --> 00:07:30,902
And it would already be diving at
over nine hundred kilometres per hour.

128
00:07:31,702 --> 00:07:35,182
Carry the same calculation on to
sixty seconds, and the trajectory

129
00:07:35,412 --> 00:07:39,122
ends up below ground level, at
well over the speed of sound.

130
00:07:40,202 --> 00:07:42,342
Obviously, that's not what would happen.

131
00:07:42,922 --> 00:07:46,822
The airplane would pass its speed limits
long before, and then hit the ground.

132
00:07:47,632 --> 00:07:50,012
And pulling out isn't instant either.

133
00:07:50,552 --> 00:07:52,972
Turning a dive back into
level flight, at one point

134
00:07:53,202 --> 00:07:56,332
eight g, costs well over
a kilometre of height.

135
00:07:56,982 --> 00:07:58,862
So the problem isn't gravity.

136
00:07:59,462 --> 00:08:01,522
Gravity would happily keep going.

137
00:08:01,982 --> 00:08:04,582
The problem is that falling
keeps making you faster...

138
00:08:05,202 --> 00:08:06,802
and you need room to stop.

139
00:08:09,472 --> 00:08:12,692
So if we plot the trajectory,
it looks something like this.

140
00:08:12,872 --> 00:08:16,172
The airplane starts
around six kilometres up.

141
00:08:16,712 --> 00:08:19,192
It climbs to around seven
point six kilometres.

142
00:08:20,088 --> 00:08:22,208
Then it enters the free-fall trajectory.

143
00:08:23,188 --> 00:08:27,098
It keeps climbing to approximately
eight point five kilometres.

144
00:08:27,428 --> 00:08:28,828
Then it starts descending.

145
00:08:29,758 --> 00:08:33,978
And somewhere during that descent, the
pilots have to pull the airplane out.

146
00:08:34,948 --> 00:08:39,548
The important part is this: the weightless
section isn't just the top of the curve.

147
00:08:40,188 --> 00:08:43,888
The aircraft is in free fall
throughout the entire ballistic arc.

148
00:08:44,478 --> 00:08:46,878
Upward. Over the top. And downward.

149
00:08:47,538 --> 00:08:51,158
That's why you don't get only a few
seconds of weightlessness at the top.

150
00:08:51,758 --> 00:08:54,578
You get roughly twenty
seconds across the whole arc.

151
00:08:56,878 --> 00:08:59,438
There's another detail
that is easy to miss.

152
00:09:00,078 --> 00:09:04,138
The change from zero g to one point
eight g isn't a sudden switch.

153
00:09:05,218 --> 00:09:07,998
The aircraft begins pulling
out of the parabola.

154
00:09:08,518 --> 00:09:10,858
The acceleration gradually increases.

155
00:09:11,538 --> 00:09:15,398
If you're floating in the cabin, you
start feeling something beneath you again.

156
00:09:16,178 --> 00:09:20,638
At first, almost nothing.
Then a little more. Then more.

157
00:09:21,278 --> 00:09:23,098
You begin moving toward the floor.

158
00:09:23,178 --> 00:09:25,638
And after several seconds, the floor is

159
00:09:25,868 --> 00:09:28,438
pushing against you with
normal gravity again.

160
00:09:29,138 --> 00:09:31,558
And then the acceleration
keeps increasing,

161
00:09:31,638 --> 00:09:34,838
until you're back at
roughly one point eight g.

162
00:09:34,918 --> 00:09:38,138
That's when being on the floor
becomes a very good idea.

163
00:09:38,928 --> 00:09:41,288
So it's not zero, then
bang, one point eight.

164
00:09:41,368 --> 00:09:45,168
It's a third of your weight.
Two thirds. Your normal weight.

165
00:09:45,398 --> 00:09:46,888
And then heavier still.

166
00:09:47,488 --> 00:09:51,508
And then you spend another twenty seconds
or so being almost twice as heavy.

167
00:09:53,928 --> 00:09:56,828
So imagine experiencing
this again and again.

168
00:09:57,348 --> 00:09:59,048
You start at normal gravity.

169
00:09:59,788 --> 00:10:01,908
Then one point eight g.

170
00:10:01,988 --> 00:10:04,668
Your body suddenly feels extremely heavy.

171
00:10:05,178 --> 00:10:06,958
Then zero g. You float.

172
00:10:07,038 --> 00:10:08,598
Your arm has no weight.

173
00:10:09,018 --> 00:10:11,018
You push yourself away from the floor.

174
00:10:11,312 --> 00:10:15,892
Then the floor slowly comes back.
Zero. Then one. Then one point eight.

175
00:10:16,228 --> 00:10:17,768
And again. And again.

176
00:10:18,188 --> 00:10:22,128
A typical parabolic flight can
perform dozens of these parabolas.

177
00:10:22,558 --> 00:10:24,378
So you're repeatedly switching between two

178
00:10:24,608 --> 00:10:26,878
completely different
physical environments.

179
00:10:27,488 --> 00:10:29,898
Almost twice your normal
apparent weight...

180
00:10:29,978 --> 00:10:31,578
and almost none.

181
00:10:32,388 --> 00:10:35,248
It's not surprising that
some people feel sick.

182
00:10:37,728 --> 00:10:40,828
The same principle can be
used to simulate other worlds.

183
00:10:41,308 --> 00:10:44,308
You don't necessarily
have to create zero g.

184
00:10:45,218 --> 00:10:47,218
The aircraft can follow
a slightly different

185
00:10:47,448 --> 00:10:49,898
trajectory and create partial gravity.

186
00:10:51,158 --> 00:10:55,358
For example, around zero point
one six g can simulate the Moon.

187
00:10:56,038 --> 00:10:59,418
And around zero point three
eight g can simulate Mars.

188
00:11:00,598 --> 00:11:02,778
Interestingly, these
phases can last longer

189
00:11:03,008 --> 00:11:05,418
than the standard microgravity parabola.

190
00:11:06,488 --> 00:11:09,768
The lower the required acceleration,
the more gradual the trajectory

191
00:11:09,998 --> 00:11:14,328
can be, allowing a longer period
of simulated partial gravity.

192
00:11:15,288 --> 00:11:18,808
So researchers can study not just
what it feels like to float...

193
00:11:18,908 --> 00:11:22,428
but what it might feel like to
move around on another world.

194
00:11:23,348 --> 00:11:25,888
Scientists run experiments on board.

195
00:11:26,408 --> 00:11:28,108
Astronauts train here.

196
00:11:28,588 --> 00:11:31,328
Film crews have used it
too: many of the weightless

197
00:11:31,558 --> 00:11:35,441
scenes in Apollo 13 were
filmed on NASA's KC-135.

198
00:11:35,688 --> 00:11:38,588
And today, members of the
public can buy a seat.

199
00:11:41,098 --> 00:11:43,998
But is it really zero
gravity? Not technically.

200
00:11:44,478 --> 00:11:46,158
Gravity is still there.

201
00:11:46,678 --> 00:11:48,778
The Earth is still
pulling on the aircraft.

202
00:11:49,518 --> 00:11:51,438
The Earth is still pulling on you.

203
00:11:51,998 --> 00:11:55,978
The reason you feel weightless is that
you and the aircraft are falling together.

204
00:11:56,918 --> 00:11:59,498
Normally, the floor
prevents you from falling.

205
00:11:59,938 --> 00:12:03,518
And that force from the floor is
what you experience as your weight.

206
00:12:04,558 --> 00:12:06,638
Remove the floor's supporting force...

207
00:12:06,718 --> 00:12:08,978
and suddenly you feel weightless.

208
00:12:10,218 --> 00:12:12,878
So perhaps the better
term isn't zero gravity.

209
00:12:13,298 --> 00:12:14,338
It's free fall.

210
00:12:14,568 --> 00:12:18,038
Or, in spaceflight terms, microgravity.

211
00:12:20,838 --> 00:12:24,758
Imagine that for your entire life,
one rule has always been obvious.

212
00:12:25,678 --> 00:12:26,978
Things fall down.

213
00:12:27,398 --> 00:12:30,798
Then, for about twenty seconds,
that rule seems to disappear.

214
00:12:31,938 --> 00:12:36,138
You raise your hand... and it floats.
You push yourself away... and you fly.

215
00:12:36,613 --> 00:12:40,653
You turn around... and nothing
tells your body which way is down.

216
00:12:41,353 --> 00:12:43,673
Then the aircraft begins pulling out.

217
00:12:44,213 --> 00:12:46,033
The floor slowly comes back.

218
00:12:46,333 --> 00:12:48,153
Your body becomes heavy again.

219
00:12:48,623 --> 00:12:50,743
And then you're back at one point eight g.

220
00:12:50,823 --> 00:12:54,243
Almost twice the force
you normally experience.

221
00:12:54,843 --> 00:12:56,363
For about twenty seconds.

222
00:12:56,703 --> 00:12:58,303
Then you're back to normal.

223
00:12:58,523 --> 00:13:01,003
And then it happens all over again.

224
00:13:03,313 --> 00:13:07,053
So a parabolic flight isn't
really about turning gravity off.

225
00:13:07,653 --> 00:13:09,893
It's about something
much more interesting.

226
00:13:11,053 --> 00:13:14,053
Using gravity itself to
make an airplane fall.

227
00:13:15,513 --> 00:13:17,213
And for about twenty seconds...

228
00:13:17,293 --> 00:13:19,143
you get to fall with it.
